  "worked_solution": "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2],$ the function must be continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $t(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $q(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=0.$  Hence, it is not continuous on $x=0.$ Therefore, $q(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $r(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=0.$ Hence, it is not continuous on $[-2,2].$ Therefore, $r(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $s(x)$ has a corner point at $x=0.$ Hence, it is not differentiable on $(-2,2).$ Therefore, $s(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $p(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Hence, it is not differentiable on $(-2,2).$  Therefore, $p(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
